#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364921 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5aaf26ff-d797-5b88-ad8d-e6959ba6a72a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364923} {:answer/id #uuid "08c96085-1bec-508c-8887-647747724f28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364924} {:answer/id #uuid "3f4ab70c-0e95-599d-840b-1e1058885e87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364925} {:answer/id #uuid "ae5c7ed9-0861-5f60-8281-0cedacc28ee1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.1\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364926} {:answer/id #uuid "56b2c2b2-4829-5aa6-968d-cb46970d071d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56b2c2b2-4829-5aa6-968d-cb46970d071d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364927} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5b0c1f8-5a20-5aeb-bf37-af86c048ed37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3e3f798-ab0c-5122-879a-c6328e19834f" :question/math-academy-id "q-103792" :question/problem "James and Mary have a date at the cinema theater.\nJames arrived at the agreed time, but Mary is late. After $20$ minutes of waiting, James decides to go home.\n\nJames travels north from the theater with a speed of $3.2\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Mary travels west toward the theater. When James is $0.8\\,\\text{km}$ from the theater, Mary is $1.5\\,\\text{km}$ from the theater. If the distance between them increases at a speed of $2.3\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is Mary's speed?" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/assets/1728c9ca91ef28b19c28048f5a640d0eebbd08f504bb594bc978277fe5620d70.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Mary and the cinema theater,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between James and the cinema theater,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between James and Mary.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=1.5\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=0.8\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {1.5}^{2}+{0.8}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =2.89 \\\\ z & =1.7\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3.2\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=2.3\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(1.5)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(0.8)(3.2) & =2(1.7)(2.3) \\\\ 3\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =7.82-5.12 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mary is approaching the cinema theater with a speed of $0.9\\,\\text{km/h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064584 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4c869f0-8036-567a-bd81-01c084217de5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36.5mph" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062016} {:answer/id #uuid "0ed857e9-fa58-5886-a6b1-82e05008ec6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28.3mph" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062017} {:answer/id #uuid "59160f73-8d4d-5948-895f-fd37f2608399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.3mph" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062018} {:answer/id #uuid "64185582-aec5-5645-8659-5b265955152a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38.1mph" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062019} {:answer/id #uuid "722e999a-c51e-510f-a34b-9968b03c9e4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.8mph" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64185582-aec5-5645-8659-5b265955152a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38.1mph" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062019} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a02cda0-1322-5984-bb34-d4827a61359e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0354de83-f7ca-5244-b85c-9f758b4f122e" :question/math-academy-id "q-2589" :question/problem "Two cars drive toward an intersection. The cars are traveling at right angles to each other, and each car's speed is $30\\,\\text{mph}.$ If car $A$ is $12$ miles from the intersection and car $B$ is $35$ miles from the intersection, how fast is the distance between the cars decreasing? Give your answer to one decimal place." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/assets/f9396718785574a63b0e1b7f621a36fee01a4d555dc67e06cf19f17bf718ba7e.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between car B and the intersection,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between car A and the intersection, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the cars.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe know that $y=12\\,\\text{mi}$ and $x=35\\,\\text{mi}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {35}^{2}+{12}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 1\\,369 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =37\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\,\\text{mph}$ when $x=35\\,\\text{mi}$ and $y=12,$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\,\\text{mph}$ when $x=35\\,\\text{mi}$ and $y=12.$\n\nNote that the rates of change are both negative because the distance to the intersection is decreasing for both cars. \n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(35)(-30)+2(12)(-30) & =2(37)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -2\\,820 & =74\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-38.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the distance between the cars is decreasing at a rate of $38.1\\,\\text{mph}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064623 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ada0a3-b73f-51cf-8002-0f7fda3adb24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364929} {:answer/id #uuid "7f551d93-1deb-5126-953a-8d1bde1f05ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,250\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364930} {:answer/id #uuid "3a29c10d-147f-573d-95e0-b67968e24fd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,350\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364931} {:answer/id #uuid "686075c8-9578-5199-b6a8-195b1d3bd42d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "975\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364932} {:answer/id #uuid "9c364074-a10d-514f-ae56-84a16b1f6156" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "750\\,\\text{m/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f551d93-1deb-5126-953a-8d1bde1f05ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,250\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364930} :answer-field/id #uuid "5150427b-4f2a-50a8-80bd-6204df660957" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "216f383a-6fa0-5f0b-b2c6-22682df9e591" :question/math-academy-id "q-2941" :question/problem "A rocket that is launched vertically is tracked by a radar station located on\nthe ground $3\\,\\text{km}$ from the launch site. What is the vertical speed of the rocket at the instant when its distance from the radar station is $5\\,\\text{km}$ and this distance is increasing at a rate of $1\\,000\\,\\text{km/h}?$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/assets/7af033f137fd76e5e3003926a598e695f4e0852b11dc27dfa84d58040144972b.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the radar station and the launch point,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the launch point and the rocket, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the radar station and the rocket.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=3\\,\\text{km}$ and $z=5\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $y$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,000\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- the distance between the radar station and the launch point ($x$) remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(3)(0)+2(4)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(5)(1\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{10\\,000}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\,250\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical speed of the rocket is $1\\,250\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc765c81-5394-5a87-bab1-58b10fe93b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364936} {:answer/id #uuid "694f3e41-260a-5587-b345-f2c44aad675f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364937}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc765c81-5394-5a87-bab1-58b10fe93b95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364936} :answer-field/id #uuid "64217037-3d78-5cdc-a08a-12fbc3e5d526" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364935}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63fd01c4-f8eb-581f-b69b-1da6de6a04d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-345644" :question/problem "Alex and Brooke agree to meet at the library. Alex arrives on time, but Brooke is late. After waiting for a while, Alex decides to leave and calls Brooke. They agree to head home instead.\n\nAlex travels north from the library at a speed of $6\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Brooke travels east away from the library. When Alex is $4\\,\\text{km}$ from the library, Brooke is $3\\,\\text{km}$ from the library. If the distance between them is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is Brooke’s speed?\n\nBrooke’s speed is \n{{field-1}}$\\text{ }\\text{km/h}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/assets/46263b1e25bc520881d590ccab503ce0d6cd33e91288e81f1d678cde9a641214.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Brooke and the library,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Alex and the library,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between Alex and Brooke.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe know that $x=3\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=4\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{2}+{4}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 9+16 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=6\\,\\text{km/h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=5\\,\\text{km/h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(3)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(4)(6) & =2(5)(6) \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+48 & =60 \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Brooke's speed is $2\\,\\text{km/h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d636c995-26da-5fbe-912b-79c440f44c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d636c995-26da-5fbe-912b-79c440f44c7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364940} :answer-field/id #uuid "889a8a7f-b966-5089-b6d1-cf3ae79f48ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cc1588f-0f0b-5749-9e0a-e178344f1239" :question/math-academy-id "q-346028" :question/problem "The length of one leg of a right triangle increases at the rate of $5\\text{ m/s},$ while the length of the second leg is decreasing at the rate of $3\\text{ m/s}.$ How fast is the length of the hypotenuse changing when the length of the increasing leg is $9\\text{ m}$ and the length of the decreasing leg is $12\\text{ m}?$\n\nThe length of the hypotenuse is changing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/assets/40fd26817dc71b5ceee1861e20a812a1835fdb92d0ff7a903d05aacc2eca2c05.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the length of the increasing side,\n\n- $y$ is the length of the decreasing side,\n\n- $z$ is the length of the hypotenuse.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe know that $x=9\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=12\\,\\text{m}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {9}^{2}+{12}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 225 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =15\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=5\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $x=9\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=12\\,\\text{m},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $x=9\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=12\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(9)(5)+2(12)(-3) & =2(15)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 18 & =30\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the hypotenuse is changing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$"}]}
